give us an idea of where negotiations  stand right now what's the state of play  as we head into another strike day  well we're in constant dialogue with the  train operating companies in one strand  of talk so with network rail in the  other  we haven't had any new offers from the  companies since the uh a few weeks ago  and in fact for the train operating  companies we've had no offer on pay  but we're continuing to talk to them  about the problems that they they want  to address the issues that what they  call modernization  at our agenda which is to get our  members of pay rise because many of them  had haven't had one for three years and  also to achieve job security but what we  need is the government  who got themselves in a peculiar  position in the last week or two as  we've all seen to change their stance  because the key to unlocking this  problem is for the Department of  Transport and the treasury to change  their stance so that we can get a bit of  good will around the negotiating table  and work up some formulas for resolving  this dispute and I hope that happens  I've met with a new secretary of state  and she you know approached that in a  positive mood we approach that in a  positive mood but we've got to see some  substantial changes  so that we can create an agreement for  all the railway workers and indeed for  the public and the taxpayer who want to  get on with getting a normal Railway  Service as do we  tell me a little bit more about that  meeting with Anne-Marie trevally in the  new transport secretary what was  different do you think about her  approach and do you think it will mean  that you can get a breakthrough  well her predecessor Grant chaps all he  did was go on media rounds and hurl  abuse at trade unionists and me  individually and other Trade union  leaders which is completely unhelpful  and he also gave the companies  uh remits that were impossible to  fulfill because even the senior  Executives in this industry who you know  aren't our friends but we work with them  know that what they were demanding was  impossible for the unions to concede to  so she at least met with us Grant chaps  refused to meet with me and the other  Union uh General secretaries she's got  that out of the way now so that's that's  pretty that that boil and we can get on  with maybe creating some solutions she's  gone into the media last week and said  that she wants to work constructively  I welcome that it doesn't mean that the  the problems are gone we've still got  difficult issues in the industry we've  got difficult issues with the structure  of the industry but we've got to remind  ourselves that the railway companies are  making profits all the time they're  living on subsidy from the taxpayer  which the uh the government is providing  and the bosses of these companies the  railway Rich are extracting millions of  pounds in their salaries and will be  benefiting from this tax break for  millionaires that quasi-quarting  provided last week so I hope that she  changes The Stance of the government and  therefore the stance of the companies  and we can get on with resolving this  dispute our members can get back to work  they can earn their living again and the  the public can expect a good service off  the railway but that's all to to be seen  in the next few weeks well yeah and I  put some of those points I spoke to um  Dan Payne's from the rail delivery agree  which represents uh rail companies in  the last hour and I put some of those  points to him about railway companies  making huge profits for example he said  that it wasn't true that they made big  profits that he said that the profits  that they made wouldn't cover a five  percent uh pay increase for workers and  on the subject of salaries he said that  it's the companies that own the rail  companies where the bosses earn earn a  lot of money what do you say to that  well the first group owns uh the railway  they own this Railway that I'm standing  outside now eventually West Coast they  are creaming in money uh the Rolling  Stock uh leasing companies paid out 75  million pound in dividend in the last  year when there was virtually nobody  traveling on the railway in 2021 because  of the covid instructions to stay at  home there is mass profit being made 500  million pounds in profit was extracted  from the railway last year between  Network rail subcontractors and the  train operators the executives of these  companies are making fortunes and  they've made fortunes since  privatization in  1993 in the mid 90s they have extracted  probably 12 billion pounds of profit  since privatization and when that gets a  bit tight when the com the country's got  difficult is all we went through the  pandemic they don't want to play the  game and what they want to do is extract  that from Railway workers they also want  to milk the public affairs on the on the  railway are extortion nobody's getting a  square deal apart from the executives  and the shareholders of these companies  they are making fortunes and they cannot  lose they have no Capital At Risk in the  new public service contracts they only  get Revenue it's like a royalty every  pound they take in fares they get a  commission from there and there is no  risk to them whatsoever that's why these  strikes are not resolved because they  get indemnified for any losses and  they're getting paid 30 million pounds  every time there is a strike so they've  probably received 200 million pound in  strike pay since this dispute commenced  it's a one-way ticket to wealth for the  super rich and that's got to end there  is a railway rich list and they are  creaming it I can assure you of that  okay and but he tells a different story  but but let's talk about the timing of  this strike as well of course this comes  a day before the London marathon it's  going to be really hard for a lot of  people to get to the starting line  tomorrow morning really disappointing  for people who's trained an awful lot  but also it's a massive charity day  isn't it this is a day that lots of  Charities rely on to raise a huge amount  of funds and they will be feeling  incredibly frustrated the timing of this  strike is is pretty awful for them  well the timing of the strike is a  succession of actions that we started  this process back in March we took our  first action in June so it's a long-term  dispute we don't want the target the  London Marathon we've got no intention  of doing that but the way the  legislation Works in this country the  anti-trade Union laws which we're going  to get more of we have to keep the  disputes going to comply with the law I  hope that the the runners and the  charity collectors and the people that  want to raise money which is a shame  they have to do that it's a shame we  haven't got our Public Services properly  funded and we have to rely on charity  instead I hope that they get to to the  starting line I hope that they could  have a good day tomorrow we don't want  to disrupt them but we have to keep this  dispute going on behalf of our members  it's also the Tory Party Conference  they've got no compunction about  um changing the way that Tax Works in  this country the way that Charities have  to raise money and our action is aimed  quite explicitly at the Tory Party  Conference which is also happening  tomorrow so this dispute will continue  and unfortunate actually there will be  disruption but if we can limit that by  through getting a settlement to the  dispute that's what we'll do and we'll  get everyone back to normal so we do  apologize for that disruption but I'm  afraid it's inevitable in a long-term  dispute whenever we go on strike  somebody or some groups will be  disrupted
 

